Start the watch mode from the dialog save

The save of the dialog spawned the write mode, which ran one pass and
stopped. A fresh install and a dead watch process therefore held no
watcher until the next login, and the GTK theme followed no later edit.

The spawn now starts the watch mode. The two locks decide what the spawn
becomes. A watch process that already runs takes the spawn to the code 0
in milliseconds. That process writes the settings, because the dialog
writes stylerc before the spawn. A session with no watch process gets
one from the spawn, and the double fork of qtcSpawn() holds it past the
life of the dialog.

The commit therefore stands on the loser behaviour of the daemon lock,
which the first two arms of assert-lock pin.

No headless check can drive the dialog of Qt, so the new check reads the
argument vector in the source. It also fails on a row of the write mode
anywhere else in the file, which stops a second spawn of that mode.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
eleventh commit. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !15 on
system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/gtk4/CMakeLists.txt b/gtk4/CMakeLists.txt
index bfb1afc2..9186d67d 100644
--- a/gtk4/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/gtk4/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -648,6 +648,13 @@ add_custom_command(TARGET qtcurve-gtk-theme POST_BUILD
           "${qtc_writer_work}/reexec"
   VERBATIM)
 
+# The save of the dialog of Qt 6 spawns the writer, and the flag of that
+# spawn is a contract of the two trees.
+add_custom_command(TARGET qtcurve-gtk-theme POST_BUILD
+  COMMAND sh "${qtc_check}/assert-dialog-spawn.sh"
+          "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp"
+  VERBATIM)
+
 # The cross check against the daemon itself, which the caller asks for. Set
 # QTC_KDED_NAMES to kded/configvalueprovider.cpp of a kde-gtk-config checkout,
 # or to the installed kded plugin gtkconfig.so of that daemon.
diff --git a/gtk4/check/assert-dialog-spawn.sh b/gtk4/check/assert-dialog-spawn.sh
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..0f627128
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gtk4/check/assert-dialog-spawn.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# assert-dialog-spawn.sh SOURCE: the save of the dialog must start the watch
+# mode of the writer. SOURCE is the source file of the dialog. The run then
+# puts the write mode back in a copy of that file, and it fails if the check
+# passes the copy.
+set -eu
+usage='usage: assert-dialog-spawn.sh SOURCE'
+src=${1:?$usage}
+
+tmp=$(mktemp -d)
+trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT
+
+# vector FILE: prints the rows of the argument vector of the spawn.
+vector() {
+    awk '/gtkWriter\[\] = \{/,/\};/' "$1"
+}
+
+# check FILE: gives 0 when the three contracts hold. It names the first
+# contract that fails on the standard error.
+check() {
+    c_file=$1
+
+    # 1. The file holds the argument vector of the spawn.
+    block=$(vector "$c_file")
+    if [ -z "$block" ]; then
+        echo "assert-dialog-spawn: $c_file holds no argument vector" >&2
+        return 1
+    fi
+
+    # 2. The vector names the watch mode.
+    case $block in
+        *'"--watch"'*) ;;
+        *) echo "assert-dialog-spawn: the vector of $c_file names no" \
+                "'--watch'" >&2
+           return 1;;
+    esac
+
+    # 3. No row of the file names the write mode, so no later edit can put a
+    # second spawn of that mode back.
+    if grep -qF '"--write"' "$c_file"; then
+        echo "assert-dialog-spawn: $c_file names '--write'" >&2
+        return 1
+    fi
+    return 0
+}
+
+if ! check "$src"; then
+    echo "assert-dialog-spawn: the source fails the check" >&2
+    exit 1
+fi
+
+# The control below breaks one contract in a copy of the source, and the check
+# must fail over that copy.
+
+# control NAME FILE: the check must fail over the file that the caller gives.
+control() {
+    name=$1
+    shift
+    if check "$@" 2> "$tmp/control.log"; then
+        echo "assert-dialog-spawn: the check passed the control copy of $name" \
+            >&2
+        exit 1
+    fi
+}
+
+# The write mode in the vector, which writes one pass and starts no watch.
+sed 's/"--watch"/"--write"/' "$src" > "$tmp/write.cpp"
+control write "$tmp/write.cpp"
+
+echo "assert-dialog-spawn: the save of the dialog starts the watch mode," \
+     "and the check rejects the control copy of the write mode"
diff --git a/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp b/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
index d39d052b..9d5f647d 100644
--- a/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
+++ b/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
@@ -1307,9 +1307,12 @@ void QtCurveConfig::save()
     qputenv(QTCURVE_PREVIEW_CONFIG, "");
 
     // qtcSpawn() reports no error, so an absent writer costs the GTK theme
-    // alone and the Qt settings still reach the disk.
+    // alone and the Qt settings still reach the disk. The spawn starts the
+    // watch mode, and a watch process that already runs makes the spawn stop
+    // at once. That process then writes the saved settings, because it
+    // watches stylerc.
     static const char *const gtkWriter[] = {
-        "qtcurve-gtk-writer", "--write", nullptr
+        "qtcurve-gtk-writer", "--watch", nullptr
     };
     qtcSpawn(gtkWriter[0], gtkWriter, nullptr, nullptr);
 }
